  <h2 id="cgt-rates-allowances">1. Capital Gains Tax Allowances & Thresholds Explained</h2>
  <p>
    When you dispose of a valuable asset that has increased in value, HMRC requires you to pay Capital Gains Tax (CGT) on the profit (the gain), not the total amount of money you receive. For many years, UK taxpayers enjoyed a relatively generous annual tax-free allowance, officially known as the Annual Exempt Amount (AEA). However, consecutive Budgets have drastically slashed this allowance.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the <strong>2025/2026 tax year</strong>, the annual tax-free <a href="/knowledge/glossary/uk-capital-gains-allowance/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">Capital Gains Allowance</a> is just <strong>£3,000</strong> for individuals and personal representatives, and a mere £1,500 for most trusts. This is a staggering drop from the £12,300 allowance available just a few years prior (2022/2023). 
  </p>
  <p>
    Because of this significant reduction, an unprecedented number of investors and property owners are now being dragged into the CGT net. If your net gains from selling shares, property, or other chargeable assets exceed £3,000 in a single tax year, you must report and pay tax on the excess.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Key Strategy:</strong> Married couples and civil partners can transfer assets between each other tax-free. This effectively means a couple can pool their allowances, shielding up to £6,000 of gains from tax in the 2025/2026 tax year.
  </p>

  <h2 id="residential-property-rates">2. Residential Property Rates & Buy-to-Let</h2>
  <p>
    The rates at which you pay CGT depend heavily on the type of asset you are selling and your Income Tax band. The UK government deliberately sets higher tax rates for residential properties that do not qualify for Principal Private Residence (PPR) relief—meaning second homes, holiday lets, and buy-to-let properties.
  </p>
  <p>
    To determine your CGT rate, you must add your total taxable capital gain (after deducting the £3,000 allowance and any allowable losses) to your taxable income. 
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Basic Rate Taxpayers:</strong> If your combined income and gains remain within the basic rate band, you pay <strong>18%</strong> on residential property gains (up from the historical 10% on standard assets).</li>
    <li><strong>Higher & Additional Rate Taxpayers:</strong> If your combined income and gains exceed the basic rate threshold, you pay <strong>24%</strong> on residential property gains. This 24% rate was recently introduced, lowered from the previous 28% peak, to stimulate the property market.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Note on Standard Assets:</strong> If you are selling standard assets like shares, cryptocurrency, or business assets, the rates are lower: 10% for basic rate taxpayers and 20% for higher/additional rate taxpayers. Business Asset Disposal Relief (formerly Entrepreneurs' Relief) can further reduce the rate to 10% up to a £1 million lifetime limit for qualifying business sales.
  </p>

  <h2 id="allowable-expenses">3. Allowable Expenses for Rental Income</h2>
  <p>
    For property investors renting out residential property, understanding exactly what can and cannot be deducted as an "allowable expense" is critical for maintaining an accurate and compliant self-assessment tax return. Allowable expenses reduce your overall rental profit, thereby reducing your Income Tax liability.
  </p>
  <p>
    HMRC makes a strict distinction between <strong>Revenue Expenses</strong> (which are allowable against rental income) and <strong>Capital Expenses</strong> (which are not allowable against income but can be offset against Capital Gains Tax when you sell the property).
  </p>
  <h3>Revenue Expenses (Deductible from Rental Income)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>General Maintenance and Repairs:</strong> Fixing a broken boiler, repainting walls between tenancies, fixing a leaky roof. Crucially, these must merely restore the property to its original state, not improve it.</li>
    <li><strong>Professional Fees:</strong> Letting agent management fees, accountancy fees for preparing your rental accounts, and legal fees for drawing up tenancy agreements or evicting tenants.</li>
    <li><strong>Insurance:</strong> Landlord insurance policies, including buildings, contents, and public liability cover.</li>
    <li><strong>Services:</strong> Wages for gardeners, cleaners, or security services that form part of the rental agreement.</li>
    <li><strong>Utilities & Council Tax:</strong> Any utility bills (water, gas, electricity) or council tax paid by the landlord during void periods (when the property is empty).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Capital Expenses (Not Deductible, but offset against CGT)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Building an extension or adding a conservatory.</li>
    <li>Upgrading a standard kitchen to a luxury, high-specification kitchen (considered an improvement rather than a repair).</li>
    <li>The initial purchase price of the property, Stamp Duty Land Tax (SDLT), and legal fees associated with the purchase.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rental-income-section-24">4. Section 24 Mortgage Relief Restrictions</h2>
  <p>
    One of the most consequential changes to UK property taxation in the last decade is Section 24 of the Finance (No. 2) Act 2015. Fully phased in as of April 2020, Section 24 fundamentally altered how individual landlords account for their mortgage interest and finance costs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Historically, landlords could deduct their full mortgage interest payments from their rental income before calculating their tax liability. Under <a href="/knowledge/glossary/uk-section-24-property-tax/" class="text-link hover:underline dark:text-link">Section 24 rules</a>, individual landlords are no longer permitted to do this. Instead, taxes are calculated on the gross rental income (minus standard allowable expenses, but not mortgage interest), and a <strong>20% basic rate tax credit</strong> is then applied based on the finance costs.
  </p>
  <p>
    This has two profound impacts:
    <br/>1. It severely diminishes the profitability of highly leveraged buy-to-let portfolios for higher and additional rate taxpayers.
    <br/>2. Because gross rental income is added to a landlord’s total income, the 'phantom income' can artificially push basic rate taxpayers into the higher tax bracket, causing them to lose child benefits or personal allowances.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because Section 24 only applies to individuals, many landlords have transitioned their portfolios into Limited Companies (Special Purpose Vehicles or SPVs), as corporate structures can still offset 100% of mortgage interest as a business expense against Corporation Tax.
  </p>

  <h2 id="60-day-reporting">5. HMRC 60-Day CGT Property Reporting</h2>
  <p>
    The administrative burden on property sellers has significantly increased. If you dispose of a UK residential property (such as a buy-to-let or a second home) and a Capital Gains Tax liability arises, you must report the sale and pay the estimated CGT to HMRC within <strong>60 days of the completion date</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This rule applies to both UK residents and non-residents. Non-residents must report the sale within 60 days even if they have no tax to pay or have made a loss. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Failure to file the UK Property Account return and pay the tax within the 60-day window triggers an immediate £100 late filing penalty. Further penalties accumulate if the delay stretches to 6 or 12 months, along with daily interest charges on the unpaid tax. It is imperative that property owners calculate their potential gains and have their financial records organized well before exchanging contracts.
  </p>

  <h2 id="let-property-campaign">6. HMRC Let Property Campaign & Disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    HMRC employs highly sophisticated data-matching systems, utilizing information from the Land Registry, letting agents, deposit protection schemes, and banks to identify individuals who are renting out property but failing to declare the income. 
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have historically undeclared rental income, HMRC provides a voluntary disclosure facility known as the <strong>Let Property Campaign</strong>. This campaign is specifically designed for residential landlords. By coming forward voluntarily rather than waiting for an HMRC investigation, landlords benefit from significantly reduced penalties and a more favorable settlement arrangement. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Ignoring undeclared income can result in penalties of up to 100% of the unpaid tax, or up to 200% for offshore non-compliance, alongside the risk of criminal prosecution.
  </p>

  <h2 id="gains-life-insurance">7. Gains on Life Insurance Policies & Shares</h2>
  <p>
    While physical property takes up much of the tax planning conversation, gains from investment portfolios, shares, and life insurance policies also require careful attention. 
  </p>
  <p>
    When a UK life insurance policy, life annuity, or capital redemption policy matures or is surrendered for a profit, it triggers a "chargeable event gain." Uniquely, these gains are not subject to Capital Gains Tax; they are subject to Income Tax. To prevent a large, one-off gain from pushing a taxpayer into a higher tax band in a single year, individuals can claim <strong>Top-Slicing Relief</strong>. This complex calculation allows the gain to be spread over the number of years the policy was held, providing a more equitable tax outcome.
  </p>
  <p>
    For shares and mutual funds outside of a tax-wrapper like an ISA or a pension, the standard £3,000 CGT allowance applies. Investors frequently utilize "Bed and ISA" strategies—selling shares up to the £3,000 CGT allowance limit and immediately repurchasing them within a tax-free Individual Savings Account (ISA) to protect future gains.
  </p>

